hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well in his book a  theory of Justice John Rawls will  identify two principles of justice that  he takes to be at the core of the theory  which he is offering as the correct one  and as a guide not just for social and  political theory but also in some other  respects for for ethics more broadly  speaking and he sets things up in such a  way that we're going to arrive at these  principles by engaging in a sort of  thought experiment or hypothetical  situation the original position or  initial situation he calls it and and  it's associated with this term the veil  of ignorance and the basic idea is that  we're deciding on fundamental principles  that are going to govern the way society  works and we're going to come to some  sort of agreement together on them  deliberatively rationally without  knowing where we would end up in that  society without knowing who we are going  to be and Rawls thinks that if we enter  into those sort of conditions we carry  out this reasoning process  in that way we're going to arrive  inevitably at two really fundamental  basic principles which then he  articulates one is as he's going to call  it early on equality and assignment of  basic rights and duties and he'll flesh  that out a little bit more and then the  second is that inequalities are only  just if they result in compensating  benefits for all not for the aggregate  of society so he was avoiding any sort  of utilitarianism here as a matter of  fact he explicitly rejects  utilitarianism he's saying that  inequalities if they're going to be just  if they're going to be right if they're  going to be acceptable have to provide  benefits for everybody in that society  so a little bit later in the the work in  Chapter eleven he titles that two  principles of justice and he he says I'm  going to state in a provisional form  these two principles that I believe  would be agreed to in the original  position and he tells us that the  formulation of these is tentative so  he's going to refine them a little bit  more in the course of the book but these  are in fact the articulations that most  people who are working off of Rawls take  to be essentially the the way to  understand these so he says here's the  way we can we can state these two  principles the first he says each person  is to have an equal right to the most  extensive scheme of equal basic  liberties compatible with a similar  scheme of liberties for others so that  requires a little bit of unpacking but  it's a fairly succinct formulation the  second he says and this one has two  different parts to it the second is that  social and economic inequalities are to  be arranged so that they are both  there's the two conditions reasonably  expected to be to everyone's advantage  and to be at-at attached to positions  and offices open to all so let's look at  these initially and then we'll look at  what Rawls actually has to say first  each person is to have an equal right to  the most extensive scheme of equal basic  liberties compatible with a similar  scheme of liberties for others so  obviously that that last part you can't  have freedom to anything whatsoever like  freedom to imprison your neighbor in the  cellar for 20 years  feeding them on apples and root  vegetables or something like that that's  ridiculous right because you're  violating their freedom in that case and  this is where we get into you know once  we get away from the general principles  when we get into the nitty-gritty it  gets quite complex right I want to  exercise my freedom to listen to my  music at 3 o'clock in the morning and I  live on a 10 acre plot my neighbors are  kind of far away how loud can I have it  cranked up you know we get into these  sort of questions Rawls isn't concerned  at this point in worrying about that but  he knows that those those situations are  going to arise sooner or later  now equal right an equal right so not  you know one person gets more than  others to the most extensive scheme the  widest possible scheme of equal basic  liberties we can also call these equal  rights so this is going to be pretty  pretty expansive and you might say well  man he sounds like a libertarian as we  see he's actually gonna be opposed to  that sort of view and it's gonna lead  more towards Anna Galit arianism of  sorts let's look at the second one now  so like we pointed out it's got two  different parts to it and it says that  there there can be social and economic  inequalities so you know it seems like  there's there's going to be some tension  here with the first but we're gonna see  how these are reconciled social and  economic inequalities can exist but they  have to be of such a nature  so that they're meeting these two  conditions now that would mean society  would have to have some basic rules  governing these things it can't just  well let any qualities grow and do  whatever they're going to do social  Darwinism Rawls would reject that sort  of view right he would say no no we can  have inequalities you can pay doctors  more than you know street sweepers but  you got to have some reasons for it so  one of the reasons is it's reasonably  expected to be to everyone's advantage  now notice he doesn't say we know with a  hundred percent certainty no reasonably  expected reasonable people can get  together and say you know if we don't  pay doctors better there may be a few  people who do it as a hobby or because  they're you know drawn to that  profession but we're probably going to  have a crisis with medicine so it would  be - everybody's advantage to actually  have doctors be compensated more highly  maybe not you know astronomically highly  then we start getting into the specifics  right now notice the other part and  attach to physicians and offices open to  all there has to be equal access equal  opportunity if we're going to have  inequalities they have to be attached to  things that that really anybody within  the society if they choose to say  undergo the training or the licensing or  whatever it's going to be that they  could have access to it they aren't just  simply being excluded so let's now look  at how Rawls goes a little bit into  detail about this and talking about the  first principle he says that the basic  liberties can be given by a list of  these sorts of liberties these include  political Liberty their right to vote  and hold public office freedom of speech  and assembly liberty of conscience that  is to say no to things or to do things  because  you have formulated you know that it's  the right thing to do  freedom of thought freedom of the person  he says which includes freedom from  psychological oppression maybe say  bullying or harassment and physical  assault and dismemberment the right to  hold personal property and freedom from  arbitrary arrest and seizure he says all  these liberties are to be equal by the  first principle if you give them to one  person you got to give them to everybody  and you've got to observe them equally  so you can't for example say well you  know stop and frisk it really is is okay  to do to people who are poor or people  who have dark skin or people who look  like they might be coming from the  Middle East or or or or right all these  sort of provisos if you're gonna do stop  and frisk you gotta apply it to  everybody equally because you're  infringing on a you know some basic  liberties freedom of arbitrary arrests  and seizure  you know perhaps freedom of thought  depending on what they're expressing  with what they're wearing and there's  all sorts of things here probably a stop  and frisk policy is not gonna pass  rauzein rules but there's all sorts of  other things that we can think about so  you know holding personal property right  should this be allowed yes Rawls says of  course doesn't mean that I can do  anything whatsoever I want with my  property because that could infringe on  the liberties of others what about the  second one he says the second principle  applies to the distribution of income  and wealth and to the design of  organizations that make use of  differences in authority and  responsibility so now we're getting to  two things that are quite important he  says well the distribution of wealth and  income need not be equal it must be -  everybody's Advaita  that's a very telling point if you want  to have a just society you can certainly  have inequalities but those inequalities  must work out to the advantage of  everybody it's hard to see how in  contemporary modern society the massive  inequalities that we have actually work  out well for say the homeless population  or the working poor or even you know the  middle class whatever that happens to be  they seem to work out quite well for  others and they would fail on Rawls's  account in that case you could say well  you know we need to encourage job  creation and innovation and bringing  products to market and all that ok if  it's really to the advantage of  everybody of everybody not just  shareholders not just those who like  getting the newest thing but to  everybody you can make a case for that  then you can have those inequalities but  those still have to be open to anybody  say then in that case entering the  market right positions he says of  authority and responsibility must be  accessible to all one applies the second  principle by holding positions open and  then subject to this constraint arranges  social and economic inequality so that  everybody benefits now notice he doesn't  just say economic inequalities he's also  saying social inequalities how we treat  people how we regard people this is a  very important we want to be able to  acknowledge the dignity of other human  beings now he's going to go on and say  that there's a priority between these  two the first takes priority over the  second and again when you when you hear  that you might say ah yeah he he sounds  like a libertarian in fact again it's  it's not that case as he goes on he says  that infringement of the basic equal  liberties protected by the first  principle cannot be justified or  compensated for by the greater social  and economic advantages these liberties  have a central ray  of application in which they could be  limited and compromised only when they  conflict with other basic liberties so  he says for example liberties not on the  list the right to own certain kinds of  property for example means of production  and freedom of contract is understood by  the doctrine of lezzie fair these are  not basic and are not protected by the  priority of the first principle so he  also says with regard to the second  principle distribution of wealth and  income and positions of authority and  responsibility must be consistent with  both the basic liberties and equality of  opportunity and then he provides us with  a sort of summary of how you can bring  these two together he says all social  values liberty and opportunity income  and wealth and the social basis of  self-respect are to be distributed  equally unless an unequal distribution  of any or all of these values is to  everyone's advantage so again we we see  that he's willing to countenance some  inequality but you've got to be able to  make a case that it is reasonably  expected to be to the advantage of all  not a merely rhetorical case about you  know job creation or it's better to be  paternalistic and not have people you  know thinking for themselves or whatever  you're going to do you you actually have  to be able to make a strong case that  the inequalities are going to be -  everybody's advantage and so these two  principles provide the skeleton of  Rawls's egalitarian conception of  justice this is applicable to any  organization to any institution to any  interaction between people provided  we're thinking things through  and it's definitely intended to provide  overarching rules or a conception of  justice for an entire society now most  societies would fail these these rules  which means that most societies are at  least to some degree  unjust and this would be helpful in  telling us how and why and to what  extent they're unjust and conversely for  following Rawls's theory what we would  need to do in order to move that society  or organization or whatever it's going  to be towards a more just condition so  this is the two principles again right  at the very center of Rawls's conception  of justice derived by considering what  rational people would do within a veil  of ignorance in which they have to work  out rules for everybody not knowing  where they would end up in that society  it would be rational Rawls says to  choose these two different principles  and to enforce them  you
 

